The code here is for analyzing a used car dataset provided in the course 
this assignment is for.

We are tasked to develope and deploy a model for predicting used car 
prices using the provided features. To do so, we will:

Explore the data provided to us
Prepare the data for modelling, including selecting and formatting the 
data. This will also include segmenting the data into a train and test set
Model the data using an appropriate frame work
Evaluate the model using the test set
Deploying the model to identify key drivers of used car prices.
 
The code does some preliminary looking through the data and then generates 
two different cleaned versions for analysis. These datasets are both split 
into a training and test set for model buidling and evaluation.

Several different models are then run to look for variables in the data 
that influence the price of a used car, these models include linear 
regression models and related models such as sequential feature selector 
and LASSO. We evaluated the models by calculating the mean squared error 
(MSE) and the mean absolute error (MAE) on both the training and test set. 

Through the models we determained the following advice:
That year is the most important 
driver of price with newer cars generally costing more. However,
a number of other factors influence price within age bands. Deseil cars 
tend to cost more than non-deseil engines. Low 
odometer millage correlates to higher prices and cars that are like new 
are priced higher. Trucks and coups generally have higher prices while
sudans and SUVs have lower prices. #Color is not the biggest determiner 
of price, but black and yellow cars tend to re-sell for more, and green 
cars tend to do very poorly.
 
To run this code you will need python 3 and scikit-learn version 1.9.0.
